    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d247">

                <pb facs="0420" n="1878" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p2/pg1878"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> S/S Files: Lot 63 D 351: NSC 102</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Executive Secretary of the National Security
                        Council</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gleason</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the National Security Council </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-19">January 19,
                        1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"> NSC 102</p>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Export Control Policy Toward the Soviet Bloc</hi>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">References: <list>
                        <label>A.</label>
                        <item> NSC 94/1<anchor n="1" xml:id="d247-f1"/>
                        </item>
                        <label>B.</label>
                        <item> NSC 92/1<anchor n="2" xml:id="d247-f2"/>
                        </item>
                        <label>C.</label>
                        <item> NSC 91/1<anchor n="3" xml:id="d247-f3"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>At the request of the Secretary of Commerce, his enclosed letter <pb facs="0421"
                        n="1879" xml:id="pg1879"/> and attached report on the subject are submitted
                    herewith for consideration at an early meeting of the National Security
                    Council.</p>
                <p>At the direction of the President, the Secretary of Agriculture,<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d247-f4"/> the Secretary of Commerce and the Economic Cooperation
                        Administrator<anchor n="5" xml:id="d247-f5"/> are being invited to
                    participate with the Council, the Secretary of the Treasury<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d247-f6"/> and the Director of Defense Mobilization<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d247-f7"/> in the consideration of this report.</p>
                <p>It is recommended that, if paragraphs 1 and 2 of the “Recommendation” in the
                    enclosure are adopted, they be submitted to the President for consideration with
                    the recommendation that he approve them and direct their implementation by all
                    appropriate executive departments and agencies of the U.S. Government.<anchor
                        n="8" xml:id="d247-f8"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Everett Gleason</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>




                <xi:include href="d247-a1.xml" xpointer="d247-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d247-a2.xml" xpointer="d247-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d247-f1" n="1" xml:id="d247-f1-content">
                <p> NSC 94/1, a report entitled “NSC Determinations Under Public Law 843, Section
                    1304 (The Cannon Amendment),” December 21, 1950, is printed in <ref
                        target="frus1950v04#pg_249">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1950, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 249</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f2" n="2" xml:id="d247-f2-content">
                <p> NSC 92/1, a report entitled “The Position of the U.S. Regarding a Blockade of
                    Trade with China,” December 21, 1950, is not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f3" n="3" xml:id="d247-f3-content">
                <p> NSC 91/1, a report entitled “East-West Trade,” November 17, 1950, is printed in
                        <ref target="frus1950v04#pg_227">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1950, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 227</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f4" n="4" xml:id="d247-f4-content">
                <p>Charles F. Brannan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f5" n="5" xml:id="d247-f5-content">
                <p>William C. Foster.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f6" n="6" xml:id="d247-f6-content">
                <p>John W. Snyder.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f7" n="7" xml:id="d247-f7-content">
                <p>Charles E. Wilson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d247-f8" n="8" xml:id="d247-f8-content">
                <p> NSC Action No. 443, taken by the National Security Council at its 84th meeting,
                    February 21, 1951, with the President presiding, recorded discussion of this
                    report, as well as of NSC 104 (extracts from which are printed on <ref
                        target="#pg1902">p. 1902</ref>), NSC 91/1, and accompanying memoranda. It
                    also referred these documents to the Special Committee on East-West Trade for
                    further study and revision in the light of the discussion at the meeting. (S/S
                    Files: Lot 62 D 1: NSC Actions)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
